choose-if: (((collectivist_relation >= 65 and collectivist_leader == "Halsmark") or (collectivist_relation >= 45 and collectivist_leader == "Conciliators")) and ((unionist_relation >= 45 and unionist_leader == "Hawkins") or (unionist_relation >= 40 and unionist_leader == "Stilling") or (unionist_relation >= 65 and unionist_ideology == "Right" and collectivist_leader != "Conciliators") or (unionist_relation >= 55 and unionist_ideology == "Right" and collectivist_leader == "Conciliators")) and ((liberal_parliament = 1 or bourgeois_parliament_ddp or lvp_formed) or ((ddp_relation >= 50 and ddp_ideology != "Left") or (ddp_relation >= 40 and ddp_ideology == "Left"))) and communist_coalition >= 3) or (unionist_leader == "Hawkins" and collectivist_leader == "Conciliators" and unionist_relation >= 35 and collectivist_relation >= 35) or (unionist_leader == "Stilling" and collectivist_leader == "Conciliators" and unionist_relation >= 30 and collectivist_relation >= 30) and president_ideology != "Right"

Could we really form a coalition that included both the [? if unionist_party_name != "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">Unionists</span>?][? if unionist_party_name == "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">**Concord**</span>?] and the <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span>? It seems like an absurd concept, but given our good relationships with both sides, it is not an impossibility.

The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span> might attempt a coup, if such a coalition were formed.

- @popular_front_2: Form the Popular Front!
- @great_popular_front: Form the Popular Front!

@popular_front_2
view-if: unionist_ideology != "Left" or collectivist_leader != "Conciliators"

Somehow, both the <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> and [? if unionist_party_name != "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">Unionists</span>?][? if unionist_party_name == "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">**Concord**</span>?] are at the very least willing to entertain negotiations. Of course, <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> radicals and [? if unionist_party_name != "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">Unionists</span>?][? if unionist_party_name == "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">**Concord**</span>?] conservatives derail the negotiations almost immediately. The <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> demands leadership of the coalition for themselves and their chairman Ernst Halsmark, [? if marcher_r > collectivist_r : despite our higher vote share, ?]while the [? if unionist_party_name != "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">Unionists</span>?][? if unionist_party_name == "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">**Concord**</span>?] is offended by the prospect of helping <span style="color: #700000;">**revolutionaries**</span> enter government. *Something* must be done to restore balance.

- @popular_front_resources
- @popular_front_democracy
- @popular_front_braun
- @coalition_menu: This is impossible. Try another governing arrangement.

