@no_majority_goerdeler_3
on-arrival: {!
Q.unionist_in_gov_mini = 1;
!}

Despite the prevailing belief that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span> have peaked, they remain a dominant force in the Assembly. Under Staal’s influence—having now betrayed Goodlake—President DuFour has appointed [+ royalist_chancellor +] as Chancellor. The cabinet remains mostly unchanged, save for Bram Frask taking over the Interior Ministry.

Staal sees this as the only viable path forward. [+ royalist_leader +] continues to pursue an all-or-nothing strategy, even after the election setback. Staal also believes he can shape cabinet policy from within, having already "convinced" Ravabelle to resign and cleared the way for his own appointment as Defense Minister.

@no_majority_goerdeler_3_alt
on-arrival: {!
Q.unionist_in_gov_mini = 0;
!}

Despite the prevailing belief that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span> have peaked, they remain a dominant force in the Assembly. Under Staal’s influence—having now betrayed Goodlake—President DuFour has appointed [+ royalist_chancellor +] as Chancellor. However, [+ unionist_leader +], staunchly opposed to serving under [+ royalist_chancellor +] as Chancellor, has withdrawn from the government and will vote against it in any confidence motion.

Staal sees this as the only viable path forward. [+ royalist_leader +] continues to pursue an all-or-nothing strategy, even after the election setback. Staal also believes he can shape cabinet policy from within, having already "convinced" Ravabelle to resign and cleared the way for his own appointment as Defense Minister.

@toleration_bruning
view-if: traditionalist_ideology == "Radical" or traditionalist_defect or concord_formed or (year = 1929 and month >= 7)
choose-if: bruning_coalition >= 50 or (marcher_toleration_ever and bruning_coalition >= 45)
unavailable-subtitle: The parties supporting Askew do not have a majority[? if marcher_toleration_ever: , and support has fallen too far for even a resumption of our old arrangement to bridge?].
on-arrival: marcher_in_government = 0; marcher_toleration = 1; marcher_toleration_ever = 1; minority_government = 1; chancellor = "Askew"; chancellor_party = "Unionist"; foreign_minister_party = "Gold"; foreign_minister_party = "LVP" if lvp_formed; interior_minister_party = "Unionist"; justice_minister_party = "I"; labor_minister_party = "Unionist"; army_minister_party = "I"; economic_minister_party = ddp_name; economic_minister_party = "LVP" if lvp_formed; finance_minister_party = "Gold"; finance_minister_party = "LVP" if lvp_formed; agriculture_minister_party = "Unionist"; gold_right += 2; ddp_right += 1; lvp_right += 2; liberal_cooperation += 0.5; left_dissent += 10 if bruning_coalition < 50; labor_dissent += 5 if bruning_coalition < 50

We have decided to tolerate Askew's government. This means that we will vote against no-confidence votes to make sure they fail, but also that we will not be directly participating as ministers in the government. Thus, we can prevent a new election and stop the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span> from entering government.

[? if bruning_coalition < 50: The old arrangement is resumed on strained numbers — Askew's supporters no longer command a true majority, and the government will survive only so long as the minor parties abstain. The party's left wing swallows the decision with open bitterness. ?]

